#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kilix-tts — read-aloud settings for Kilix, as a screen and as a CLI.

Everything this screen edits lives in the shared GPU Terminal settings file, and
everything it tests goes through ``kilix-voiced``.  Both halves of that matter:
the tab-bar speaking-head button reads the same file, so the two can never
disagree about what "read this" means, and a test phrase travels the same
route — daemon, arbiter, engine, sink — that a real read takes, so a test that
sounds right proves the real path rather than a private shortcut.

The screen and ordinary audible tests leave synthesis and playback to the
daemon. File exports and explicit --interactive sessions run a local
synthesiser directly; only the latter opens an audio device. A machine with no
daemon running and no ``espeak-ng`` installed still opens the screen, still
saves settings, and says in the status block what is missing and what to install
rather than failing to start.

As a CLI, for scripts and for terminals that cannot run curses:

    kilix-tts --print
    kilix-tts --status
    kilix-tts --set wpm=200 --set voice=en-gb
    kilix-tts --speak "Hello from Kilix"
    printf '%s' "Longer text" | kilix-tts --speak - --model espeak
    kilix-tts --speak "Save this" --output speech.wav
    kilix-tts --interactive --model espeak
"""

from __future__ import annotations

